    "version": "time-de-1.0",
    "note": "German mirror of the 24-item probe. The English half is byte-identical to data/tasks_time_v2.json, so English acts as the same within-item ceiling in every language file and they can be pooled. German is the falsification test, not another replication. German counts INTO the coming hour as Russian does, but only with a closed set of three lexical items (halb, viertel, dreiviertel); there is no productive German form for arbitrary minutes, so 07:20 can only be zwanzig nach sieben. Direction of counting is held constant and productivity is varied. The memorisation reading predicts German into_hour is read CORRECTLY, because three frequent phrases are memorisable where a rule is not, and therefore that German looks like English throughout. The rival reading, that counting into the coming hour is simply hard, predicts German into_hour fails like Russian. German is also high-resource in every corpus, which removes the resource explanation without needing to measure anything. Two items (g07, g09) are flagged de_regional: viertel neun and viertel zwei are used in the east and south and may not parse for a northern speaker, so their gold label is contested among natives and they must be excluded from any primary comparison unless deliberately included. viertel is lowercase throughout on purpose, so that viertel nach fuenf and viertel neun differ by one word and not also by capitalisation. WORDING NOT YET CHECKED BY A NATIVE SPEAKER — see tools/make_german_probe.py."
  },
